TOPIC 1 : INTRODUCTION TO AGILE & AGILE MANIFESTO
======================================================================

Q1. What is Agile methodology, and why was it introduced?

Interview Ready Answer

Agile is a software development methodology based on iterative and
incremental development. It focuses on delivering working software in
small increments, encouraging continuous stakeholder collaboration,
and adapting to changing requirements.

It was introduced to overcome the limitations of the Waterfall model,
where the product is delivered only after all phases are completed and
requirement changes become expensive.

Definition

Agile is a software development methodology that emphasizes iterative
development, incremental delivery, customer collaboration, and
continuous improvement.

Purpose

• Deliver working software frequently.
• Adapt to changing business requirements.
• Encourage continuous customer collaboration.
• Reduce project risk through early feedback.
• Deliver business value faster.

----------------------------------------------------------------------
Q2. What are the four values of the Agile Manifesto?

Interview Ready Answer

The four values of the Agile Manifesto are:

1. Individuals and interactions over processes and tools.
2. Working software over comprehensive documentation.
3. Customer collaboration over contract negotiation.
4. Responding to change over following a plan.

Key Points

• Agile does NOT reject the items on the right.
• It simply values the items on the left more.

----------------------------------------------------------------------
Q3. Do we need to memorize all 12 Agile Manifesto principles?

Interview Ready Answer

No.

For campus interviews, it is generally not necessary to memorize all
12 principles verbatim. However, you should understand the core ideas
behind them, such as:

• Customer collaboration
• Responding to changing requirements
• Frequent delivery of working software
• Continuous improvement
• Team collaboration

If specifically asked, explain the principles at a high level.

----------------------------------------------------------------------
Q4. What are the key differences between Agile and Waterfall?

Interview Ready Answer

Agile follows an iterative and incremental development approach,
delivers working software frequently, encourages continuous customer
collaboration, and easily adapts to changing requirements.

Waterfall follows a sequential approach where each phase is completed
before the next begins. Customer involvement is limited after
requirements are finalized, changes are difficult to incorporate, and
the product is generally delivered at the end of the project.

Purpose

Agile

• Iterative and Incremental
• Frequent software releases
• Continuous customer collaboration
• Easy to accommodate changes
• Continuous feedback

Waterfall

• Sequential development
• Single final delivery
• Limited customer involvement
• Requirement changes are costly
• Feedback mainly at the end

======================================================================
INTERVIEW TRAP QUESTIONS
======================================================================

Q1. Is Agile a framework?

Answer:
No. Agile is a methodology (or approach). Scrum is a framework that
implements Agile principles.

------------------------------------------------------------------

Q2. Does Agile eliminate documentation?

Answer:
No. Agile values working software more than comprehensive
documentation, but documentation is still important.

------------------------------------------------------------------

Q3. Does Agile eliminate planning?

Answer:
No. Agile supports planning, but it also allows plans to change when
business requirements change.

------------------------------------------------------------------

Q4. Do we need to memorize all 12 principles?

Answer:
No. Understand the concepts behind them. Memorizing all 12 is usually
not expected in campus interviews.

